Now the controller. Fingerprint: for transfer, canonical evidence = sorted joined keys. Controller computes:

```java
String canonical = request.evidenceKeys().stream().sorted().collect(Collectors.joining(","));
String hash = idempotencyAdvisor.hash(CaseTransferService.OP_CASE_TRANSFER, actorId, canonical, request);
```

The request object also contains evidenceKeys (unsorted) — serialized as-is. Since canonical string is included, [A,B] vs [B,A] produce same hash only if the rest of request serializes identically — but evidenceKeys list differs in order → different JSON → different hash! That breaks normalization. So I should hash a normalized request, not the raw request. Options: build a normalized copy of the request with sorted keys for hashing:

```java
CaseTransferRequest normalized = new CaseTransferRequest(request.commandKey(), ..., sortedKeys);
String hash = idempotencyAdvisor.hash(OP, actorId, canonical, normalized);
```

That gives identical hashes for [A,B] and [B,A]. Good.

